The assertion is refuted because the term "red-tagging" and its practice in the Philippines actually date back to the mid-1950s as a local application of US McCarthyism, as documented by [BBC News](https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-54144623). Linguistically, it is the Philippine equivalent of the global term "red-baiting," which refers to making groundless accusations of communism to discredit individuals, according to [RationalWiki](https://rationalwiki.org/wiki/Red-baiting). Furthermore, the Supreme Court of the Philippines defined the term in a 2015 ruling, tracing its definition back to a 2011 academic journal, as documented on [Wikiwand](https://www.wikiwand.com/en/Red-tagging_in_the_Philippines). There is no verifiable evidence that the CPP-NPA-NDF coined the term, and the claim's author failed to provide any proof during Senate hearings, as reported by [PressOne](https://pressone.ph/pcoos-badoy-claims-sc-junked-red-tagging-case-filed-by-nujp-journos-ask-what-case/).